                "description": "FFWJKL\n\nThe National Weather Service in Jackson has issued a\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nNortheastern Bell County in southeastern Kentucky...\nNorthwestern Harlan County in southeastern Kentucky...\nSoutheastern Leslie County in southeastern Kentucky...\nSouthwestern Letcher County in southeastern Kentucky...\n\n* Until 630 PM EDT.\n\n* At 330 PM EDT, Doppler radar indicated thunderstorms producing\nheavy rain across the warned area. Up to 2.5 inches of rain have\nfallen. Flash flooding is ongoing or expected to begin shortly.\n\nHAZARD...Flash flooding caused by thunderstorms.\n\nSOURCE...Radar indicated.\n\nIMPACT...Flash flooding of small creeks and streams, urban areas,\nhighways, streets and underpasses as well as other poor\ndrainage and low-lying areas.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nHarlan, Loyall, Wallins Creek, Rosspoint, Golden Ash, Kitts,\nClovertown, Baxter, Harlan Gas, Bledsoe, Napier, Putney, Sunshine,\nBig Laurel, White Star, Pine Mountain, Tremont, Nolansburg, Arthur\nand Kentenia.\n\nThis includes the following streams and drainages...\nLynn Fork, Oldhouse Branch, Elk Creek, Laurel Fork, Fourmile\nBranch, Catron Creek, Line Fork, Jesses Creek, Martins Fork\nCumberland River, Salt Trace Branch, Stony Fork, Jakes Creek,\nLaurel Branch, Wallins Creek, Foresters Creek, Big Run, Little\nLaurel Creek, Big Branch, Poor Fork Cumberland River, Terry Fork,\nLewis Creek, Cumberland River, Gabes Creek, Ages Creek, Rockhouse\nCreek, Clover Fork, Clover Fork Cumberland River, James York\nBranch, Middle Fork Kentucky River, Right Fork Bill Branch, Ben\nHoward Branch, Stoney Fork, Straight Creek, Lovely Branch, Greasy\nCreek, Reuben Branch, Beech Fork and Bailey Creek.",
